<h2 local-id=3D"e9cc210d-46dd-4b8b-9a3d-52a55d3a38d0" id=3D"ReleaseManageme=
nt-Summary"><img class=3D"emoticon emoticon-blue-star" data-emoji-id=3D"1f5=
2e" data-emoji-shortname=3D":crystal_ball:" data-emoji-fallback=3D"=F0=9F=
=94=AE" src=3D"30055d102f15c3e4868e7fb2185efcdbe1da3cf69929a5d23b5ae55df422=
7078" width=3D"16" height=3D"16" data-emoticon-name=3D"blue-star" alt=3D"(b=
lue star)">&nbsp;Summary</h2>
<p local-id=3D"e88a0939-678b-4e04-954c-8e690be2fb5d">The Dynamics CRM Relea=
se Management process uses four key templates to ensure structured and cont=
rolled deployments. The <strong>Release Template</strong> captures essentia=
l details such as change requests, Jira links, release plans, solution comp=
onents, deployment steps, rollback strategies, and approvals. The <strong>C=
hange Plans Template</strong> outlines the type of plan, its description, i=
mpact, risk, and schedule, typically following a monthly or bi-monthly cade=
nce. The <strong>Communication Plan Template</strong> identifies teams, sta=
keholders, segment managers, and the Change Advisory Board for effective co=
ordination. Finally, the <strong>Post-Implementation Review Template</stron=
g> documents the post deployment smoke testing results (if applicable). The=
 post-release testing and sign-off maybe a requirement by the CAB to close =
the Change Request. This could also be beneficial with follow-up actions to=
 improve future releases.</p>
